.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library implements the Simple Two-way Active Measurement Protocol
(STAMP, RFC 8762) with the Session-Sender Identifier and the
Location, Timestamp Information, Class of Service and Direct
Measurement TLVs of RFC 8972:
its packets in either mode, unauthenticated and authenticated (RFC
8762 section 4.4 and the HMAC TLV of RFC 8972 section 4.8), its
timestamps and error estimate, the clock's synchronisation, the
Session-Reflector's core, the Session-Sender's core and the UDP socket
of either end.
It depends on the C library and, for the HMAC, on libcrypto.
Of the TLVs that follow the base packet, the Location, Timestamp
Information, Class of Service and Direct Measurement TLVs are filled
by the reflector and read by the sender, the HMAC TLV made and checked
in the authenticated mode, the others marked unrecognised.
.Ss Packets
.Fn stamp_test_encode
writes a Session-Sender test packet
.Pq Vt struct stamp_test : No sequence number, timestamp, error estimate, SSID
into the first
.Dv STAMP_UNAUTH_LEN
(44) octets of
.Fa buf ,
its MBZ octets zero, what follows them as it is;
.Fa len
must be at least 44.
.Fn stamp_test_decode
reads one;
.Fn stamp_reflected_decode
reads a Session-Reflector's
.Pq Vt struct stamp_reflected ,
the sender's fields it echoes among them and whether its MBZ octets
were zero.
Each timestamp is read in the format its error estimate's Z bit says.
.Fn stamp_tlv_next
returns the TLV at
.Fa *off
(from the end of the base packet) and moves
.Fa *off
past it: 1, 0 when none is left, or \-1 with
.Va errno
.Er EBADMSG
when one runs past the packet.
.Fn stamp_tlv_put
writes a TLV of
.Fa type
and
.Fa vlen
octets of
.Fa value
(NULL: what is there left as it is) at
.Fa *off ,
its flags 0, and moves
.Fa *off
past it; \-1 with
.Er EMSGSIZE
when it does not fit.
.Fn stamp_tsinfo_find
reads a reflected packet's Timestamp Information TLV
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_TSINFO , No RFC 8972 section 4.3
into
.Vt struct stamp_tsinfo :
the reflector's clock's synchronisation source
.Pq Dv STAMP_SYNC_NTP , STAMP_SYNC_PTP , STAMP_SYNC_SSU , STAMP_SYNC_GNSS , STAMP_SYNC_LOCAL
and how it took T2
.Pq Va sync_in , ts_in
and T3
.Pq Va sync_out , ts_out ;
the methods
.Dv STAMP_TSM_HW , STAMP_TSM_SW , STAMP_TSM_CP .
It returns 1, 0 when there is none or the reflector marked it
unrecognised or malformed, \-1 when the TLVs run past the packet.
.Fn stamp_cos_find
reads the Class of Service TLV
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_COS , No RFC 8972 section 4.4
into
.Vt struct stamp_cos ,
the same way:
.Va dscp1 ,
the DSCP the sender asked the reply to carry;
.Va dscp2
and
.Va ecn ,
those the request reached the reflector with; and
.Va rp ,
1 when the reflector would not set
.Va dscp1 ,
its reply carrying the request's DSCP instead.
.Fn stamp_dm_find
reads the Direct Measurement TLV
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_DM , No section 4.5
into
.Vt struct stamp_dm :
.Va s_tx ,
the packets the sender sent in the session, that one with them;
.Va r_rx
and
.Va r_tx ,
those the reflector received and sent in it; 0 too when the reflector
counted none
.Pq Va r_rx No 0 , No a stateless one .
.Fn stamp_location_find
reads the Location TLV
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_LOCATION , No section 4.2
into
.Vt struct stamp_location :
.Va src
and
.Va dst ,
the address and port the request came from and to as the reflector saw
them, from its Source and Destination IPv4 or IPv6 Address sub-TLVs;
one not said (zero, the reflector hiding it or not knowing) of family
0; 0 too when its sub-TLVs run past it.
.Fn stamp_location_nat
says what of that was rewritten on the way:
.Dv STAMP_NAT_SRC
when the source is not
.Fa self ,
the address and port this end sent from (a port of 0 not compared),
.Dv STAMP_NAT_DST
when the destination is not
.Fa far ,
the address and port it sent to; NULL, or what the TLV does not say,
not compared.
.Ss Timestamps and the error estimate
.Fn stamp_ntp_put
and
.Fn stamp_ntp_get
convert a
.Vt timespec
of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
to and from NTP's 64-bit format (RFC 5905), its era told by the top
bit of the seconds as RFC 4330 does;
.Fn stamp_ptp_put
and
.Fn stamp_ptp_get ,
PTPv2's truncated format (seconds and nanoseconds), whose epoch is
the caller's to choose.
.Fn stamp_ts_put
and
.Fn stamp_ts_get
choose by an error estimate's
.Dv STAMP_EE_Z
bit.
.Fn stamp_ts_sub
is
.Fa a
\-
.Fa b
in seconds.
.Pp
.Fn stamp_ee_make
makes an error estimate (RFC 4656 section 4.1.2) of
.Fa err
seconds: the S bit when
.Fa synced ,
Z when
.Fa ptp ,
the least scale whose multiplier fits an octet, the multiplier
rounded up and never 0.
.Fn stamp_ee_seconds
is the error an estimate says.
.Fn stamp_ee_clock
is the clock's,
.Fn stamp_clock_get Ns 's .
.Pp
.Fn stamp_clock_get
says the clock
.Pq Vt struct stamp_clock :
its error estimate and its synchronisation source, from
.Xr ntp_gettime 2 :
synchronised unless its state is
.Dv TIME_ERROR ,
then by
.Dv STAMP_SYNC_NTP ,
else
.Dv STAMP_SYNC_LOCAL
(free-running); the error its maximum error.
.Fn stamp_clock_set
says it instead, for the process, for a clock that
.Xr ntp_gettime 2
does not know is synchronised:
.Fa spec
is
.Ql source Ns Op : Ns Ar error ,
the source
.Cm ntp , ptp , ssu , gnss
or
.Cm local
(not synchronised), the error in seconds (default the least an error
estimate says); NULL goes back to
.Xr ntp_gettime 2 .
It returns 0, or \-1 with
.Er EINVAL .
.Fn stamp_sync_name
and
.Fn stamp_tsm_name
name a source and a method,
.Ql -
for 0.
.Ss The Session-Reflector
.Fn stamp_refl_new
makes a reflector of
.Vt struct stamp_refl_conf :
.Bl -tag -width rate_total
.It Va stateful
The reply's sequence number counts the packets of its session (RFC 8762
section 4.3.2) rather than copying the sender's; a session is the
caller's tag (a FIB), the sender's address and port, the address the
packet came to and the SSID.
.It Va rate , burst
Replies a second to one source, and the burst its token bucket allows
(0: twice the rate); a rate of 0 sets no limit.
A source is an IPv4 address (an IPv4-mapped IPv6 address alike) or an
IPv6 /64.
The buckets are a table of 4096, four ways a set, keyed by a random
secret, a new source taking its set's oldest.
.It Va rate_total
Replies a second to all sources, its burst as many; 0 none.
A request whose HMAC verified draws from buckets apart, its source's
and a total of its own as large, so that unauthenticated requests,
whose source addresses anybody writes, do not starve the authenticated
ones.
A request of the authenticated layout takes a token from a bucket of
its source's of its own, of this rate and burst, before any HMAC is
made, given back when the HMAC verifies: failures past the rate are
dropped before an HMAC
.Pq Dv STAMP_DROP_PREAUTH ,
those answered never use it up.
Whoever writes a sender's address can drain it, as it can that
address's bucket of unauthenticated requests, but not the buckets of
the requests verified; no total of such tokens is kept.
.It Va sess_max , sess_idle
Sessions kept at most
.Pq Dv STAMP_SESS_MAX , No 4096
and the seconds one ends after without a packet
.Pq Dv STAMP_SESS_IDLE , No 300 ;
0 for those.
A session made by an authenticated request is apart from an
unauthenticated one of the same key.
When the table is full the oldest made unauthenticated is replaced, an
authenticated one only by another authenticated request and when there
is none made unauthenticated; an unauthenticated request then makes
none and is not answered
.Pq Dv STAMP_DROP_FULL .
.It Va port
The reflector's own port: a packet from it is not answered, nor one
from port 0.
.It Va ts_out
How the caller takes T3, for the Timestamp Information TLV; 0 for
.Dv STAMP_TSM_SW .
.It Va dscp_deny
The DSCPs a Class of Service TLV may not ask the reply to carry, bit
.Ar n
for DSCP
.Ar n :
their replies carry the DSCP the request came with, the TLV's RP 1.
.It Va loc_hide
A Location TLV's ports and addresses are not said (zero), as RFC 8972
lets a reflector's policy.
.It Va keys , nkeys
The keys of the authenticated mode, copied
.Pq at most Dv STAMP_KEYS_MAX : No see Sx The authenticated mode :
at most
.Dv STAMP_KEYS_SSID_MAX
(4) an SSID, tried in their order, and at most
.Dv STAMP_KEYS_ANY_MAX
(4) for any SSID, tried after them; more, and
.Fn stamp_refl_new
and
.Fn stamp_refl_setconf
fail with
.Er E2BIG ,
an SSID past 65535 with
.Er EINVAL ,
and nothing is changed.
.It Va auth_only
A request that is not of the authenticated layout is not answered
.Pq Dv STAMP_DROP_UNAUTH .
.El
.Pp
.Fn stamp_refl_setconf
changes it, its sessions kept while it stays stateful.
.Fn stamp_refl_reply
answers a packet as it came
.Pq Vt struct stamp_rx : No the packet, the sender's address, the address it came to, the TTL it came with, the time it came Pq T2 ,
the session's tag and a monotonic time (0: now) into
.Fa out
and returns the reply's length, always the packet's: what follows the
base is copied.
When that is TLVs to its end, none of type 0, the Timestamp
Information TLV is filled (the clock's source,
.Va ts_in
of the
.Vt struct stamp_rx
for T2, 0 for
.Dv STAMP_TSM_SW ,
and
.Va ts_out
for T3), the Class of Service TLV too (DSCP2 and ECN from
.Va tos ,
the TOS or traffic class octet the request came with, \-1 not known),
the Direct Measurement TLV (the session's requests received, one over
a rate limit among them, and replies sent; 0 each when stateless) and
the Location TLV (the reflector's port, or the one
.Va local
says, and the sender's; each sub-TLV asking for the source or
destination address answered in the packet's family, the destination
the address it came to, zero when
.Va local
is NULL; one asking for the source MAC answered as an EUI-64 of zero,
no UDP socket seeing it; another marked unrecognised); one of those
not as long as its kind is (Timestamp Information 4 or more, Class of
Service 4, Direct Measurement 12, Location 4 or more) or whose sub-TLVs
run past it marked malformed
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_M ,
and any other but Extra Padding marked unrecognised
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_U ;
anything else is padding, copied as it is.
.Fn stamp_refl_reply_tos
is the TOS octet the reply just made is to be sent with: the Class of
Service TLV's DSCP1, or, refused by
.Va dscp_deny ,
the request's DSCP; \-1 (the socket's own) when the request had no
such TLV, or its TOS is not known.
A packet shorter than the base, from port 0 or the reflector's own,
over a rate limit, authenticated with no key that verifies it, not
authenticated to a reflector authenticated only, not authenticated
to a stateful one whose sessions are all authenticated, or
authenticated past its source's rate before its HMAC, is not
answered: \-1, why in
.Fa *why
.Pq Dv STAMP_DROP_SHORT , STAMP_DROP_PORT , STAMP_DROP_RATE , STAMP_DROP_AUTH , STAMP_DROP_UNAUTH , STAMP_DROP_FULL , STAMP_DROP_PREAUTH ,
counted.
The caller sets the reply's own timestamp (T3) as late as it can with
.Fn stamp_refl_finish ,
which makes an authenticated reply's HMAC after it, and sends it;
.Fn stamp_refl_send_time
sets an unauthenticated one's alone.
.Fn stamp_refl_expire
ends the sessions idle for
.Va sess_idle ;
.Fn stamp_refl_stats
says the packets answered, those dropped by reason, those answered
whose HMAC TLV was wrong
.Pq Va tlv_failed
and the sessions.
.Ss The Session-Sender
.Fn stamp_sender_new
makes a sender of
.Vt struct stamp_sender_conf ,
one session to one reflector:
.Bl -tag -width timeout
.It Va ssid
The Session-Sender Identifier its packets carry and its replies must.
.It Va seq_set , seq0
The first sequence number when
.Va seq_set ;
else a random one, never 0, so that the first reply tells a stateful
reflector (whose new session counts from 0) from a stateless one.
.It Va mode
The reflector's mode when known
.Pq Dv STAMP_MODE_STATELESS , STAMP_MODE_STATEFUL ;
.Dv STAMP_MODE_UNKNOWN
for the first reply to tell.
.It Va timeout
Seconds a reply is waited for (0: 2); one later is lost.
.It Va window
Packets in flight at most (0: 256).
.It Va tsinfo
The packets carry the Timestamp Information TLV for the reflector to
fill.
.It Va cos , dscp
The packets carry the Class of Service TLV, asking the reflector to
send its replies with DSCP
.Va dscp ;
the caller sends them with the DSCP it wants
.Pq Dv IP_TOS , IPV6_TCLASS
and reads the DSCP the replies came with
.Pq Fn stamp_recv .
.It Va dm
The packets carry the Direct Measurement TLV, their count in the
session, for the loss each way by counters.
.It Va location
The packets carry the Location TLV, asking for the destination and
source addresses: what a NAT on the way rewrote.
.It Va owd_frac
The delay each way is said one way when the two error estimates
together are no more than this part of half the round trip
.Pq 0: Dv STAMP_OWD_FRAC , No a tenth .
.El
.Pp
Like the reflector's, the core does no I/O: the caller sends and
receives, each time on the monotonic clock
.Pq Fn stamp_mono , No or Dv STAMP_SOCK_MONOTONIC Ns 's receive time .
.Fn stamp_sender_packet
writes the next test packet into
.Fa buf ,
its timestamp
.Fa t1
(the time of day), sent at
.Fa mono ;
it returns 0, or 1 when the window was full and its oldest packet
given up, lost, that one's sample in
.Fa *lostp .
With any of
.Va tsinfo , cos , dm
or
.Va location ,
the Timestamp Information, Class of Service, Direct Measurement and
Location TLVs follow the base, in that order, empty but for DSCP1, the
count of packets sent and the sub-TLVs asking for the addresses, and
an Extra Padding TLV the rest,
.Fa len
as
.Fn stamp_sender_len
says: the base's 44 octets at least, 8 more for each of the first two
TLVs, 16 for the Direct Measurement TLV and 48 for the Location TLV,
and with more after them 4 octets or more
.Pq else Er EMSGSIZE ;
.Fn stamp_sender_len
is that length for one of
.Fa want
octets.
.Fn stamp_sender_reply
matches a reply that came at
.Fa mono ,
and at
.Fa t4
on the time of day (NULL: not known), to its packet by the SSID and
the sender's sequence number it echoes:
1 with its sample in
.Fa *out ,
0 for none of the session's packets in flight (another SSID, a reply
late or again), \-1 for no reflected packet.
A sample
.Pq Vt struct stamp_sample
has the round trip with the reflector's time taken out,
.Va rtt
= (T4 \- T1) \- (T3 \- T2), T1 and T4 monotonic and T2 and T3 both the
reflector's, so that neither clock need be right;
.Va refl
is T3 \- T2;
.Va fwd
and
.Va back
the delay each way, T2 \- T1 and T4 \- T3 with T1 and T4 on the time of
day when
.Va owd :
both ends' error estimates say synchronised (S), their errors
together
.Pq Va err
no more than
.Va owd_frac
of half the round trip, and neither way negative; otherwise half the
round trip each, an estimate;
.Va has_tsinfo
and
.Va tsinfo
the reflector's Timestamp Information TLV;
.Va has_cos
and
.Va cos
its Class of Service TLV;
.Va has_dm
and
.Va dm
its Direct Measurement TLV, when it counted, with
.Va s_rx
the replies received here, that one with them;
.Va has_loc
and
.Va loc
its Location TLV; and
.Va r
the reply.
.Fn stamp_sender_expire
gives up the oldest packet in flight past its timeout: 1 with its
sample
.Pq Va lost ,
0 when there is none; it is called until 0.
.Fn stamp_sender_next
is when the oldest packet in flight times out, 0 with none.
.Pp
.Fn stamp_sender_stats
counts
.Pq Vt struct stamp_sender_stats
the packets sent, received and lost, the replies late (after their
timeout, or again) and foreign, and the reflector's mode, told by its
first reply.
A stateful reflector's sequence number counts what reached it, so
the losses between two replies are told apart:
.Va fwd_lost
never reached the reflector,
.Va back_lost
were reflected but not back in time.
A stateless reflector's are the round trip's alone,
.Va unknown_lost ,
as are those before the first reply and those across a counter that
went back or leapt (the reflector started again).
The losses between two replies are counted once every packet before
the later one is answered or lost, up to a timeout after; those after
the last reply are in
.Va lost
alone.
With
.Va dm ,
the Direct Measurement TLV's counters tell the loss each way too,
summed from the first reply that has them
.Pq Va dm_told :
between two such replies, the packets sent less those the reflector
received were lost on the way out
.Pq Va dm_fwd_lost ,
the replies it sent less those received here on the way back
.Pq Va dm_back_lost , No a late one among them ;
a reply before one sent earlier is passed over, and counters that went
back (the reflector started again) start the next part of the sum.
With
.Va key ,
the sender's packets are of the authenticated mode under it (a copy):
.Va auth_failed
counts the replies passed over, not of that layout or whose HMAC is
wrong (tampered with, or made under another key), their packets lost
when their time comes;
.Va tlv_failed
those whose TLVs were not used, their HMAC TLV wrong or flagged
.Dv STAMP_TLV_I
by the reflector.
.Fn stamp_mode_name
names a mode.
.Ss The authenticated mode
A packet of the authenticated mode is
.Dv STAMP_AUTH_LEN
(112) octets of base, its fields apart with MBZ octets between them (RFC
8762 sections 4.2.2 and 4.3.2, the SSID at octet 26 as RFC 8972 has
it) and its last 16 an HMAC of the first 96: HMAC-SHA-256 truncated to
128 bits
.Pq Dv STAMP_HMAC_LEN .
Nothing but TLVs may follow it, well-formed to the packet's end, and
after them, Extra Padding aside, the HMAC TLV
.Pq Dv STAMP_TLV_HMAC , No type 8
over the Sequence Number and the TLVs before it; Extra Padding TLVs
alone need none.
A sender's padding is an Extra Padding TLV.
A key
.Pq Vt struct stamp_key : No its octets, 16 to 64, and the SSID it is for, or Dv STAMP_SSID_ANY
is the session's.
.Pp
The reflector answers a request of that layout
.Pf ( Fn stamp_auth_layout :
112 octets or more, the twelve after the Sequence Number zero, where
the unauthenticated layout has its timestamp) only when its HMAC
verifies under one of its SSID's keys, or else under one of any
SSID's, the first that does, one HMAC each, eight at most; its reply
is of the same
layout under the same key.
A request whose HMAC no key verifies, or after whose base come octets
that are not well-formed TLVs to its end (left over, a header cut
short, a length past the packet, a TLV of type 0), is dropped and
counted
.Pq Dv STAMP_DROP_AUTH ,
never answered; one whose TLVs are well-formed but whose HMAC TLV is
wrong, missing or out of its place is answered with its TLVs as they came, each flagged
.Dv STAMP_TLV_I
.Pq RFC 8972 section 4.8 ,
the HMAC TLV made again.
One whose MBZ was rewritten on the way no longer looks authenticated:
it is answered as an unauthenticated request, unless
.Va auth_only ,
and the authenticated sender takes no such reply.
Every HMAC is compared in constant time
.Pq Xr CRYPTO_memcmp 3 .
.Pp
.Fn stamp_hmac
is that HMAC of
.Fa a
then
.Fa b
.Pq Fa blen No 0: Fa a No alone
into
.Fa out ;
.Fn stamp_auth_verify
and
.Fn stamp_auth_seal
check and make a base packet's,
.Fn stamp_auth_tlv_seal
makes the HMAC TLV of a packet whose TLVs start at
.Fa base ;
.Fn stamp_auth_tlv_verify
checks them, failing closed: 1 when the HMAC TLV is right, or nothing
but Extra Padding TLVs (or nothing) follows the base; 0 when the TLVs
are well-formed and their HMAC TLV wrong, missing or out of its place;
\-1 when what follows the base is not well-formed TLVs to its end.
Its walk is
.Fn stamp_tlv_next Ns 's ,
so the cores act on exactly what it checked.
.Fn stamp_auth_ssid
reads the SSID.
.Fn stamp_auth_narrow
moves a packet's fields, in place, where the unauthenticated layout has
them, its TLVs after them, 68 octets shorter;
.Fn stamp_auth_widen
the reverse, its MBZ and HMAC zero;
.Fa refl
for a reflected packet's.
.Fn stamp_auth_send_time
sets a reflected packet's timestamp.
.Pp
.Fn stamp_keys_parse
reads keys from text, a line each:
.Dq Ar ssid key ,
the SSID 0 to 65535 or
.Ql *
for any, the key 32 to 128 hexadecimal digits;
.Ql #
begins a comment.
An SSID has
.Dv STAMP_KEYS_SSID_MAX
keys at most (a key rotated is two keys of its SSID for a while, the
old and the new), and
.Dv STAMP_KEYS_ANY_MAX
at most are for any; one more for an SSID, or for any, is an error.
An error says the line's number, never a key.
.Fn stamp_keys_read
reads them from an open file, from its start: a regular file its owner
alone may read and write, owned by root or by the process's user.
It is read whole or not at all: an error reading it, fewer octets than
its size, or a size or modification time changed while it was read
fail it, never a smaller set of keys.
.Fn stamp_keys_free
zeroes the keys and frees them.
.Ss Sockets
.Fn stamp_socket
opens a non-blocking UDP socket of family
.Fa af ,
bound to
.Fa port
(0: any) on every address, an IPv6 one
.Dv IPV6_V6ONLY ,
in the process's FIB
.Pq Xr setfib 2 ;
the time a packet comes is the kernel's
.Pq Dv SO_TIMESTAMP , SO_TS_REALTIME ,
the TTL or hop limit, the TOS or traffic class
.Pq Dv IP_RECVTOS , IPV6_RECVTCLASS
and the address it came to said.
.Dv STAMP_SOCK_REFLECTOR
in
.Fa flags
sends with TTL 255;
.Dv STAMP_SOCK_MONOTONIC
has the kernel's receive time on the monotonic clock
.Pq Dv SO_TS_MONOTONIC ,
a sender's.
.Fn stamp_socket_fib
opens it in FIB
.Fa fib
.Pq Dv SO_SETFIB , No before it is bound ;
\-1 for the process's.
.Fn stamp_recv
reads a packet and what came with it
.Pq Vt struct stamp_rxinfo :
.Va t
the time of day it came,
.Va mono
the monotonic time, each the kernel's when it said, the other then
that instant on its clock, else the clock's;
.Va hwts
when the kernel says the hardware took it
.Pq Dv SCM_TIME_INFO ;
.Va tos
the TOS or traffic class octet it came with, \-1 not said.
.Fn stamp_send
sends from the address
.Fa from
(the one a packet came to), the routes' own when that is not of an
interface of the socket's FIB, with the TOS or traffic class octet
.Fa tos
.Pq Dv IP_TOS , IPV6_TCLASS ; No \-1 the socket's .
.Fn stamp_sockaddr
resolves a host and port;
.Fn stamp_sa_port
and
.Fn stamp_sa_ntop
read a socket address.
